  "notes": "いらいら is a mimetic word ({擬態語|ぎたいご}) expressing irritation, frustration, or nervous agitation. It can function as an adverb, noun, or with する as a verb.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: {自動詞|じどうし} (intransitive)\n- No standard transitive pair\n- Pattern: いらいらする\n\nASPECT (ている):\n- いらいらする: to get/feel irritated (event)\n- いらいらしている: to be in an irritated state (ongoing)\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- いらいらする: to be irritated\n- いらいらさせる: to irritate someone\n- いらいらを⟦{抑|おさ}える→抑える：12806_osaeru⟧: to suppress one's irritation\n- いらいらが⟦{募|つの}る→募る：10878_tsunoru⟧: irritation builds up\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- ⟦むかむか→むかむか：05265_mukamuka⟧: anger or nausea — more visceral\n- ⟦もやもや→もやもや：05984_moyamoya⟧: vague unease or dissatisfaction\n- ⟦うんざり→うんざり：04829_unzari⟧: fed up, tired of something\n\nWRITING:\nAlso commonly written in katakana as イライラ, especially in casual writing or manga.",
